\description{
A \strong{logmu} mortality is an object class that defines the annual mortality
rate \eqn{\mu_{it}} for individual \eqn{i} at time \eqn{t}, where
\eqn{i} contains all the available invariant information relating to the
relevant individual.

All \strong{logmu} actually works in terms of \eqn{\log\mu_{it}} for reasons set out
TBC.

Access to \eqn{\log\mu_{it}} for a mortality object is implicit using
\code{.i} and \code{.t} pronouns.

For testing and understanding, you can access the \eqn{\log\mu_{it}}
calculation by defining your own \code{.i} and \code{.t} variables and then calling
\code{log_mu()} on a mortality. Do \emph{not} use this for performant scenarios.

These are the core types of mortality object:\tabular{lll}{
   Type \tab \strong{datey} function \tab Notes \cr
   Constant \tab \code{\link[=mortality_const]{mortality_const()}} \tab  \cr
   Age-period \tab \code{\link[=mortality_table]{mortality_table()}} \tab Mortality rates that are smooth at an annual scale \cr
   Gompertz \tab \code{\link[=gompertz_mortality]{gompertz_mortality()}} \tab Log mu linear in age and in time \cr
   Model \tab TBC \tab  \cr
}


You can build on these:
\itemize{
\item Add a projection to a \code{\link[=base_table]{base_table()}} to make an age-period table, see
\link{projection_table}. Scale a base table with \code{\link[=q_scaled]{q_scaled()}} or \code{\link[=mu_scaled]{mu_scaled()}}.
\item Select between tables using TBC.
\item Scale mortality rates using TBC.
\item Provide sub-annual mortality variation using TBC.
}

LINK TO OTHER PACKAGES TO GET STANDARD TABLES

Finally there are a couple of
mortality-related helper functions:
\itemize{
\item \code{is_mortality(x)} tests whether \code{x} is a \code{mortality}.
\item \code{terminal_age(x)} gets the terminal age of a \code{\link[=mortality_table]{mortality_table()}} or a
\code{\link[=base_table]{base_table()}}, i.e. the age by which everyone is assumed to have died.
Any other \code{mortality} may have a terminal age that depends on the
individual, so use \code{terminal()}.
\item \code{terminal(x, .i)} gets the date by which each individual in \code{.i} is
assumed to have died. A \code{mortality} with no terminal age, such as a constant
or a Gompertz, gives \code{datey::datey(datey::valid_years_end)}. An expression
that refers to a \code{\link[=mortality_table]{mortality_table()}} is not yet supported.
}

Terminal ages and dates are provided for valuation calculations and are
currently ignored for A/E calculations.

Builds a \code{mortality} from a pronoun expression for \eqn{\log\mu}. Concrete
\code{mortality} objects may appear in the expression -- each contributes its own
\eqn{\log\mu} at the individual and time -- so you can
\itemize{
\item select between mortalities with \code{ifelse()} (the condition must be
time-invariant, as for any conditional),
\item scale or adjust a mortality (adding in \eqn{\log\mu} is multiplying
\eqn{\mu}), or
\item write a closed-form law directly from the pronouns.
}

A bare reference to a concrete \code{mortality} is returned unchanged.
}
